  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nA slightly drier air mass briefly moves into the region on Friday,\nbut it will be short lived as the axis of higher moisture is\nexpected to arrive Friday night and Saturday in association with\nthe tropical wave. This is reflected in PoPs jumping up into the\n30-50% range over SE Florida Friday night/Saturday morning, then \n60-80% over interior and SW Florida Saturday afternoon as the wave\nmoves west and E/SE wind flow steers convection away from the east\ncoast and towards the west coast. A few of these thunderstorms on\nSaturday could be rather strong as the SAL layer superimposed on \nthe higher moisture at low levels could steepen lapse rates just \nenough. \n\nThe tropical wave will be west of the area by Saturday night,\nwhich will open the door for a larger SAL to move into the region\non Sunday. Model consensus PWATs impressively drop to the 1.4-1.6\ninch range on Sunday which is the lowest 10th percentile for mid-\nAugust. Therefore, PoPs are essentially less than 10% area-wide on\nSunday, which of course is quite rare for this time of year. PWATs\nmodestly increase Monday and Tuesday to about the 30 to 40th\npercentile, which supports PoPs in the 30-50% which are still a \nlittle below seasonal norms.\n\nAside from a slight decrease in temperatures on Saturday due to\nincreased cloud cover and precipitation, temperatures and heat\nindex will remain high enough for the possibility of heat advisory\nconditions to continue. The hottest temperatures of the long \nterm period are expected on Sunday when the lowest PoPs should \ncoincide with warmer temperatures aloft, resulting in highs in the\nmid to upper 90s over most of South Florida. Some dry air could \nmix down to the surface and lower dewpoints just enough to \npreclude a widespread significant heat episode, but temperatures \nshould still be high enough for moderate to major HeatRisk.",
